This topic is about ensuring that data entering your pipelines is accurate, consistent, and useful, and then enhancing it to deliver greater business value.

From the session aim :

You are expected to:

Identify data quality issues
Apply cleansing techniques
Enrich datasets using additional data
Instructor Insight

At EPA level, this is a critical shift:

Pipelines don’t fail because of code — they fail because of bad data

2. Real-World Case – Airline Loyalty Failure

From page 3 :

A global campaign failed due to:

18% invalid/missing emails
12% duplicate IDs
Data spread across multiple systems
What this actually shows

This is not just a “data issue” — it is a pipeline failure upstream:

No validation rules
No deduplication
No standardisation
Business Impact
Campaign ROI dropped by 40%
Applied Understanding

At EPA level, you should say:

“Poor data quality directly impacts business outcomes by reducing the effectiveness of downstream analytics and operations.”

3. What is “Bad Data”? (Foundation Concept)

From page 10 :

Bad data includes:

Incomplete values
Inconsistent formats
Inaccurate or invalid entries
Duplicate records
Applied Explanation

The key insight is:

Even small inconsistencies can break entire pipelines

Example from the bad CSV diagram (page 9):

Mixed country formats (“UK”, “United Kingdom”)
Missing fields
Poor formatting
Instructor Insight

This links directly to earlier topics:

Bad data → breaks ingestion → breaks analytics → breaks decisions

4. Identifying Data Quality Issues

From the diagram on page 11 :

Six core issues include:

Inconsistency
Inaccuracy
Invalid data
Duplication
Incomplete data
Imprecision
Applied Understanding

A strong data engineer doesn’t wait for errors — they:

Profile data early
Identify patterns of failure
Design pipelines to handle them
EPA-Level Thinking

“I implemented validation and profiling steps to detect inconsistencies early in the pipeline.”

5. Standardisation (Making Data Usable)

From page 12 :

Standardisation ensures:

Consistent formats
Normalised categories
Uniform structure
Applied Example
Dates → ISO format
Country → “UK” → “United Kingdom”
Text → consistent casing
Why this matters

Without standardisation:

Joins fail
Aggregations break
Analysis becomes unreliable
Instructor Insight

Standardisation enables integration across systems

6. Deduplication (Ensuring One Source of Truth)

From page 13 :

Techniques include:

Exact matching
Fuzzy matching
Unique identifiers
Applied Example

From the slide:

“123 Main St” vs “123 Main Street”
Real Understanding

Deduplication is not just removal — it is:

Identifying duplicates
Deciding how to merge
Preserving correct information
EPA Insight

A strong answer explains trade-offs:

“Fuzzy matching improves accuracy but increases computational complexity.”

7. Validation (Preventing Errors Early)

From page 14 :

Validation includes:

Data type checks
Range checks
Constraint enforcement
Cross-field consistency
Applied Understanding

Validation should happen:

At ingestion, not after failure

Example
Email format validation
Age must be > 0
Date must be valid
Instructor Insight

This directly links to the earlier “silent failure” scenario:

Validation prevents pipelines from “running but failing”

8. Data Enrichment (Adding Value)

From page 16 :

Enrichment adds new, meaningful context to existing data

Applied Techniques
Internal joins (e.g. customer status)
External APIs (e.g. weather, demographics)
Derived fields (e.g. age, spend metrics)
Key Insight

Cleansing fixes data — enrichment makes it valuable

EPA-Level Answer

“I enriched the dataset using external APIs to provide additional context for analysis.”

9. Integrating External Data

From page 17 :

Methods include:

APIs (real-time)
Static files (batch)
Streaming feeds
Applied Understanding

Each method has trade-offs:

APIs → flexible but latency-dependent
Files → stable but delayed
Streams → real-time but complex
Instructor Insight

This links back to ingestion patterns (Module 7):

You must choose integration method based on use case

10. Hackathon (Where This Becomes REAL)

Now we bring in the supporting file because it adds real EPA-level application.

From the hackathon brief :

Part A – Pipeline Design

You must:

Combine sunrise/sunset + weather data
Generate minute-level temperature data (~527,040 records)
What this is REALLY testing

Not just pipeline building — but:

Data transformation logic
Handling missing/interpolated values
Documenting assumptions
Critical Applied Insight

From the brief:

You must assume a linear trend between daily min/max temperatures

This is data enrichment + modelling:

You are generating new data from incomplete data
EPA-Level Thinking

A strong answer would say:

“I derived minute-level temperature values by interpolating between daily minimum and maximum readings, ensuring consistency and documenting assumptions.”

Part B – Pipeline Reuse (VERY IMPORTANT)

From the brief :

You must:

Use someone else’s pipeline
Adapt it to new data
Handle schema differences
What this tests

This is one of the most important real-world skills:

Pipelines must be reusable and adaptable

Applied Insight

This links directly to:

Schema evolution
Data validation
Pipeline robustness
EPA-Level Answer

“I adapted the pipeline to handle schema differences by updating validation rules and transformation logic.”

Part C & D – Advanced Thinking (Extension)

These go beyond cleansing into:

Modelling relationships
Linking data to business outcomes
Detecting anomalies
Key Insight

This is where:

Data engineering → data science → business insight

11. What This Topic Is REALLY Testing

